<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-category-theory">
<title>Category Theory</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>Functions and composition from <xref ref="sec-functions" />, relations from
<xref ref="sec-relations" />, abstract algebra from <xref ref="sec-abstract-algebra" />,
group theory from <xref ref="sec-group-theory" />, ring theory from
<xref ref="sec-ring-theory" />, module theory from <xref ref="sec-module-theory" />,
universal algebra from <xref ref="sec-universal-algebra" />, and lattice theory from
<xref ref="sec-lattice-theory" />.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>explain why category theory studies mathematical structures through
    objects and structure-preserving maps;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define categories, objects, morphisms, identities, and composition;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>interpret standard examples such as <m>\mathbf{Set}</m>,
    <m>\mathbf{Grp}</m>, <m>\mathbf{Ring}</m>, and <m>\mathbf{Vect}_F</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize isomorphisms, monomorphisms, and epimorphisms;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish initial, terminal, and zero objects;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>construct product and coproduct objects through universal properties;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define functors and natural transformations;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish covariant and contravariant constructions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>interpret opposite categories;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand equivalence of categories;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>explain the basic idea of representable functors;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>state the Yoneda lemma and explain its conceptual significance;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define adjunctions and recognize their relation to universal
    constructions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand limits and colimits as generalizations of familiar
    constructions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>identify the role of category theory throughout modern mathematics.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</remark>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-why-category-theory">
<title>Why Category Theory?</title>
<p>Much of mathematics studies objects equipped with structure.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>sets;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>groups;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>rings;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>fields;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>vector spaces;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>modules;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>topological spaces;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>partially ordered sets;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>graphs;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>manifolds.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>For each kind of object, mathematics also studies maps that preserve the
relevant structure.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>

<p>Category theory studies the common structure behind these situations.</p>

<p>\begin{conceptbox}
Category theory shifts attention from the internal elements of mathematical
objects to the relationships among objects expressed through maps.
\end{conceptbox}</p>

<p>This perspective makes it possible to identify patterns that occur
simultaneously throughout algebra, topology, geometry, analysis, logic, and
other branches of mathematics.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-objects-morphisms">
<title>Objects and Morphisms</title>
<p>The two basic ingredients of a category are <em>objects</em> and
<em>morphisms</em>.</p>

<p>A morphism is an abstract arrow between objects.</p>

<p>If <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are objects, a morphism from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m> is written</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B.</md>
</p>

<p>This is read <q>@@PROTECTED0@@ is a morphism from @@PROTECTED1@@ to @@PROTECTED2@@.</q></p>

<p>The word <em>morphism</em> comes from the idea of a structure-preserving map,
although morphisms in an abstract category need not literally be functions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-definition">
<title>Definition of a Category</title>
<definition>
<title>Category</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>category</em> <m>\mathcal C</m> consists of:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>a collection of objects;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>for each pair of objects <m>A,B</m>, a collection of morphisms
    from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>for every object <m>A</m>, an identity morphism</p>

<p>
<md>1_A:A\to A;</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>a composition operation for compatible morphisms.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>Composition must be associative and identity morphisms must act as identities.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal C</md>
</p>

<p>is a calligraphic capital <m>C</m>, commonly used to denote a category.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-hom-sets">
<title>Hom-Sets</title>
<p>The collection of morphisms from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m> is commonly denoted</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C}(A,B).</md>
</p>

<p>This is read <q>Hom from @@PROTECTED0@@ to @@PROTECTED1@@ in the category @@PROTECTED2@@.</q></p>

<p>When the category is understood, one often writes simply</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Hom}(A,B).</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>f\in\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C}(A,B)</md>
</p>

<p>means that <m>f</m> is a morphism</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-categorical-composition">
<title>Composition</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B,\qquad g:B\to C.</md>
</p>

<p>Then their composite is a morphism</p>

<p>
<md>g\circ f:A\to C.</md>
</p>

<p>The expression</p>

<p>
<md>g\circ f</md>
</p>

<p>is read <q>@@PROTECTED0@@ composed with @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<p>As with ordinary function composition, the map <m>f</m> acts first and <m>g</m>
acts second.</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}
A \arrow[r,"f"] \arrow[rr,bend right=25,"g\circ f"'] &amp;
B \arrow[r,"g"] &amp;
C
\end{tikzcd}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-associativity">
<title>Associativity of Composition</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B,\qquad g:B\to C,\qquad h:C\to D,</md>
</p>

<p>then category composition must satisfy</p>

<p>
<md>h\circ(g\circ f)=(h\circ g)\circ f.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore an unambiguous composite may be written</p>

<p>
<md>h\circ g\circ f.</md>
</p>

<p>Diagrammatically,</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}
A \arrow[r,"f"] &amp;
B \arrow[r,"g"] &amp;
C \arrow[r,"h"] &amp;
D
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>both possible parenthesizations produce the same morphism from <m>A</m> to
<m>D</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-identity-morphisms">
<title>Identity Morphisms</title>
<p>For every object <m>A</m>, there is an identity morphism</p>

<p>
<md>1_A:A\to A.</md>
</p>

<p>It satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>1_B\circ f=f</md>
</p>

<p>for every</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>f\circ1_A=f.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus identity morphisms behave like identity functions.</p>

<p>Some texts use</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{id}_A</md>
</p>

<p>instead of <m>1_A</m>.</p>

<p>This is read <q>the identity on @@PROTECTED0@@.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-network">
<title>A Category as a Network</title>
<p>A useful first picture of a category is a network of objects connected by
morphisms.</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large,row sep=large]
A \arrow[r,bend left=15,"f"] \arrow[dr,"h"'] &amp;
B \arrow[l,bend left=15,"g"] \arrow[d,"k"]\\
&amp;
C
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>The objects are nodes.</p>

<p>The morphisms are arrows.</p>

<p>Unlike an ordinary graph, however, a category also contains composites of
compatible arrows and an identity arrow at every object.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-set">
<title>The Category of Sets</title>
<p>The most familiar category is</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{Set}.</md>
</p>

<p>Its objects are sets and its morphisms are functions.</p>

<p>Thus if <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are sets,</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbf{Set}}(A,B)</md>
</p>

<p>is the collection of functions from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m>.</p>

<p>Composition is ordinary function composition, and the identity morphism is
the ordinary identity function.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-grp">
<title>The Category of Groups</title>
<p>The category</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{Grp}</md>
</p>

<p>has:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>groups as objects;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>group homomorphisms as morphisms.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbf{Grp}}(G,H)</md>
</p>

<p>is the set of group homomorphisms</p>

<p>
<md>G\to H.</md>
</p>

<p>The composition of group homomorphisms is again a group homomorphism.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-algebraic-categories">
<title>Other Algebraic Categories</title>
<p>Many algebraic structures naturally form categories.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>

<p>Notation varies among authors, but the underlying idea is the same.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-top">
<title>The Category of Topological Spaces</title>
<p>The category</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{Top}</md>
</p>

<p>has topological spaces as objects and continuous functions as morphisms.</p>

<p>This example demonstrates that category theory is not restricted to algebra.</p>

<p>Later chapters will use categorical ideas to organize constructions in
topology, geometry, and analysis.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-posets-as-categories">
<title>Posets as Categories</title>
<p>Every poset</p>

<p>
<md>(P,\leq)</md>
</p>

<p>can be regarded as a category.</p>

<p>The elements of <m>P</m> are objects.</p>

<p>There is a unique morphism</p>

<p>
<md>x\to y</md>
</p>

<p>exactly when</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq y.</md>
</p>

<p>Reflexivity supplies identity morphisms, and transitivity supplies
composition.</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}
x \arrow[r] &amp; y \arrow[r] &amp; z
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>corresponds to</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq y\leq z,</md>
</p>

<p>which implies</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq z.</md>
</p>

<p>This explains why many order-theoretic ideas have categorical analogues.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-groups-one-object-category">
<title>Groups as One-Object Categories</title>
<p>A group can also be interpreted categorically.</p>

<p>Take a category with one object, say <m>*</m>.</p>

<p>Let the morphisms</p>

<p>
<md>*\to*</md>
</p>

<p>be the elements of a group <m>G</m>.</p>

<p>Composition is group multiplication.</p>

<p>The identity morphism is the group identity.</p>

<p>Because every group element has an inverse, every morphism is invertible.</p>

<p>\begin{conceptbox}
A group can be viewed as a category with exactly one object in which every
morphism is an isomorphism.
\end{conceptbox}</p>

<p>This is an important example of how category theory reorganizes familiar
algebraic structures.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-categorical-isomorphisms">
<title>Isomorphisms</title>
<definition>
<title>Isomorphism</title>
<statement>
<p>A morphism</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B</md>
</p>

<p>is an <em>isomorphism</em> if there exists a morphism</p>

<p>
<md>g:B\to A</md>
</p>

<p>such that</p>

<p>
<md>g\circ f=1_A,\qquad f\circ g=1_B.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The morphism <m>g</m> is the inverse of <m>f</m>.</p>

<p>One writes</p>

<p>
<md>A\cong B</md>
</p>

<p>when <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are isomorphic.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-isomorphism-diagram">
<title>Isomorphism Diagram</title>
<p>An isomorphism may be visualized as</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=huge]
A \arrow[r,bend left=18,"f"] &amp;
B \arrow[l,bend left=18,"g"]
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>with</p>

<p>
<md>g\circ f=1_A,\qquad f\circ g=1_B.</md>
</p>

<p>Categorically, isomorphic objects possess the same structure relevant to the
category.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-isomorphism-versus-equality">
<title>Isomorphism Versus Equality</title>
<p>Two objects can be isomorphic without being literally equal.</p>

<p>For example, the sets</p>

<p>
<md>A=\{1,2,3\},\qquad B=\{a,b,c\}</md>
</p>

<p>are different sets but are isomorphic in <m>\mathbf{Set}</m> because there is
a bijection between them.</p>

<p>Likewise, two vector spaces may contain completely different elements yet
be isomorphic as vector spaces.</p>

<p>\begin{importantbox}
Category theory generally treats isomorphic objects as structurally
equivalent even when they are not literally identical.
\end{importantbox}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-monomorphisms">
<title>Monomorphisms</title>
<p>Category theory generalizes injectivity without referring directly to
elements.</p>

<definition>
<title>Monomorphism</title>
<statement>
<p>A morphism</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B</md>
</p>

<p>is a <em>monomorphism</em> if for every pair</p>

<p>
<md>g,h:X\to A,</md>
</p>

<p>the equality</p>

<p>
<md>f\circ g=f\circ h</md>
</p>

<p>implies</p>

<p>
<md>g=h.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>A monomorphism is often abbreviated <em>mono</em>.</p>

<p>It is sometimes written</p>

<p>
<md>A\hookrightarrow B.</md>
</p>

<p>The hooked arrow <m>\hookrightarrow</m> often suggests an injective or
embedding-like morphism, although notation depends on context.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-epimorphisms">
<title>Epimorphisms</title>
<p>The dual concept is an epimorphism.</p>

<definition>
<title>Epimorphism</title>
<statement>
<p>A morphism</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B</md>
</p>

<p>is an <em>epimorphism</em> if for every pair</p>

<p>
<md>g,h:B\to X,</md>
</p>

<p>the equality</p>

<p>
<md>g\circ f=h\circ f</md>
</p>

<p>implies</p>

<p>
<md>g=h.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>An epimorphism is often abbreviated <em>epi</em>.</p>

<p>A common notation is</p>

<p>
<md>A\twoheadrightarrow B.</md>
</p>

<p>The double-headed arrow <m>\twoheadrightarrow</m> commonly indicates a
surjective or quotient-like morphism.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-categorical-cancellation">
<title>Cancellation Interpretation</title>
<p>Monomorphisms and epimorphisms can be remembered through cancellation.</p>

<p>A monomorphism permits left cancellation:</p>

<p>
<md>f\circ g=f\circ h\Longrightarrow g=h.</md>
</p>

<p>An epimorphism permits right cancellation:</p>

<p>
<md>g\circ f=h\circ f\Longrightarrow g=h.</md>
</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large]
X \arrow[r,shift left=3pt,"g"] \arrow[r,shift right=3pt,"h"'] &amp;
A \arrow[r,"f"] &amp;
B
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>If <m>f</m> is monic, equality after composing with <m>f</m> forces <m>g=h</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-monic-not-injective">
<title>Monic Does Not Always Mean Injective</title>
<p>In <m>\mathbf{Set}</m>, monomorphisms are exactly injective functions.</p>

<p>In many familiar algebraic categories, monomorphisms are also injective
homomorphisms.</p>

<p>However, the categorical definition does not mention elements.</p>

<p>Similarly, epimorphisms need not always be surjective.</p>

<p>For example, in the category of rings with identity and identity-preserving
homomorphisms, the inclusion</p>

<p>
<md>\Z\hookrightarrow\Q</md>
</p>

<p>is an epimorphism even though it is not surjective.</p>

<p>This illustrates why categorical concepts must be understood through their
universal behavior rather than only through familiar set-theoretic models.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-initial-objects">
<title>Initial Objects</title>
<definition>
<title>Initial Object</title>
<statement>
<p>An object <m>I</m> in a category <m>\mathcal C</m> is <em>initial</em> if for every
object <m>A</m>, there exists exactly one morphism</p>

<p>
<md>I\to A.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Diagrammatically,</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}
I \arrow[r,dashed,"\exists!"'] &amp; A
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\exists!</md>
</p>

<p>is read <q>there exists a unique.</q></p>

<p>In <m>\mathbf{Set}</m>, the empty set</p>

<p>
<md>\varnothing</md>
</p>

<p>is initial because there is exactly one function</p>

<p>
<md>\varnothing\to A</md>
</p>

<p>for every set <m>A</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-terminal-objects">
<title>Terminal Objects</title>
<definition>
<title>Terminal Object</title>
<statement>
<p>An object <m>T</m> is <em>terminal</em> if for every object <m>A</m>, there exists
exactly one morphism</p>

<p>
<md>A\to T.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>In <m>\mathbf{Set}</m>, every singleton set is terminal.</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>T=\{*\},</md>
</p>

<p>then every set <m>A</m> has exactly one function</p>

<p>
<md>A\to\{*\}.</md>
</p>

<p>It sends every element of <m>A</m> to <m>*</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-unique-up-to-isomorphism">
<title>Uniqueness up to Unique Isomorphism</title>
<p>Initial and terminal objects need not be literally unique.</p>

<p>Instead, they satisfy a stronger structural principle.</p>

<p>\begin{theorem}
Any two initial objects in a category are uniquely isomorphic.
Likewise, any two terminal objects are uniquely isomorphic.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>\begin{proof}
Let <m>I</m> and <m>J</m> be initial.</p>

<p>Since <m>I</m> is initial, there is a unique morphism</p>

<p>
<md>f:I\to J.</md>
</p>

<p>Since <m>J</m> is initial, there is a unique morphism</p>

<p>
<md>g:J\to I.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>g\circ f:I\to I.</md>
</p>

<p>But there is exactly one morphism <m>I\to I</m>, and <m>1_I</m> is such a
morphism. Hence</p>

<p>
<md>g\circ f=1_I.</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>f\circ g=1_J.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore <m>I\cong J</m>.
\end{proof}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-zero-objects">
<title>Zero Objects</title>
<definition>
<title>Zero Object</title>
<statement>
<p>An object is a <em>zero object</em> if it is both initial and terminal.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>A zero object is commonly denoted</p>

<p>
<md>0.</md>
</p>

<p>In <m>\mathbf{Grp}</m>, the trivial group is a zero object.</p>

<p>In <m>\mathbf{Vect}_F</m>, the zero vector space is a zero object.</p>

<p>The category <m>\mathbf{Set}</m> does not have a zero object because the empty
set is initial but not terminal.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-universal-properties">
<title>Universal Properties</title>
<p>One of the central ideas of category theory is the <em>universal
property</em>.</p>

<p>Instead of describing an object solely through its internal construction,
one characterizes it through the unique maps connecting it to all relevant
objects.</p>

<p>\begin{conceptbox}
A universal property describes a mathematical object by how morphisms
uniquely factor through it.
\end{conceptbox}</p>

<p>Initial objects and terminal objects are the simplest examples.</p>

<p>Products, coproducts, kernels, quotients, tensor products, free objects, and
many other constructions can also be described universally.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-categorical-products">
<title>Categorical Products</title>
<definition>
<title>Product</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>product</em> of objects <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> is an object <m>A\times B</m>
together with morphisms</p>

<p>
<md>\pi_A:A\times B\to A,\qquad
\pi_B:A\times B\to B</md>
</p>

<p>such that for every object <m>X</m> with morphisms</p>

<p>
<md>f:X\to A,\qquad g:X\to B,</md>
</p>

<p>there exists a unique morphism</p>

<p>
<md>\langle f,g\rangle:X\to A\times B</md>
</p>

<p>making the corresponding diagram commute.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The lowercase Greek letter</p>

<p>
<md>\pi</md>
</p>

<p>is <em>pi</em>, pronounced <q>pie.</q></p>

<p>Here <m>\pi_A</m> and <m>\pi_B</m> are called projection morphisms.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-product-universal-diagram">
<title>Product Universal Diagram</title>
<p>The defining diagram is</p>

<p>\begin{centereddiagram}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large,row sep=large]
&amp; X \arrow[dl,"f"'] \arrow[d,dashed,"{\exists!\,\langle f,g\rangle}"] \arrow[dr,"g"] &amp; \\
A &amp; A\times B \arrow[l,"\pi_A"] \arrow[r,"\pi_B"'] &amp; B
\end{tikzcd}
\end{centereddiagram}</p>

<p>Commutativity means</p>

<p>
<md>\pi_A\circ\langle f,g\rangle=f,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\pi_B\circ\langle f,g\rangle=g.</md>
</p>

<p>The universal property determines the product uniquely up to unique
isomorphism.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-products-familiar-categories">
<title>Products in Familiar Categories</title>
<p>In <m>\mathbf{Set}</m>, the categorical product is the Cartesian product.</p>

<p>In <m>\mathbf{Grp}</m>, it is the direct product of groups.</p>

<p>In <m>\mathbf{Vect}_F</m>, the product of finitely many vector spaces agrees
with their direct sum.</p>

<p>Thus several familiar constructions are instances of one categorical idea.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-categorical-coproducts">
<title>Coproducts</title>
<p>The dual construction is the coproduct.</p>

<definition>
<title>Coproduct</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>coproduct</em> of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> is an object <m>A\amalg B</m> equipped
with morphisms</p>

<p>
<md>\iota_A:A\to A\amalg B,\qquad
\iota_B:B\to A\amalg B</md>
</p>

<p>such that for every pair</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to X,\qquad g:B\to X,</md>
</p>

<p>there exists a unique morphism</p>

<p>
<md>[f,g]:A\amalg B\to X</md>
</p>

<p>making the defining diagram commute.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\amalg</md>
</p>

<p>is often read <q>coproduct</q> or <q>disjoint union.</q></p>

<p>The lowercase Greek letter</p>

<p>
<md>\iota</md>
</p>

<p>is <em>iota</em>, pronounced <q>eye-oh-tuh.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-coproduct-universal-diagram">
<title>Coproduct Universal Diagram</title>
<p>\begin{centereddiagram}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large,row sep=large]
A \arrow[r,"\iota_A"] \arrow[dr,"f"'] &amp;
A\amalg B \arrow[d,dashed,"{\exists!\,[f,g]}"] &amp;
B \arrow[l,"\iota_B"'] \arrow[dl,"g"]\\
&amp; X &amp;
\end{tikzcd}
\end{centereddiagram}</p>

<p>The diagram commutes when</p>

<p>
<md>[f,g]\circ\iota_A=f,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>[f,g]\circ\iota_B=g.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-coproducts-familiar">
<title>Coproducts in Familiar Categories</title>
<p>Coproducts depend strongly on the category.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>

<p>The same universal pattern therefore produces different concrete
constructions in different categories.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-worked-category-product">
<title>Worked Example: Product in <m>\mathbf{Set}</m></title>
<example>
<title>The Cartesian Product as a Categorical Product</title>
<p>Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be sets and suppose</p>

<p>
<md>f:X\to A,\qquad g:X\to B.</md>
</p>

<p>Construct the unique map <m>X\to A\times B</m>.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{\langle f,g\rangle(x)=(f(x),g(x)).}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-commutative-diagrams">
<title>Commutative Diagrams</title>
<p>Category theory frequently expresses equations through diagrams.</p>

<p>A diagram is <em>commutative</em> if every directed path with the same starting
and ending objects gives the same composite morphism.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large,row sep=large]
A \arrow[r,"f"] \arrow[dr,"h"'] &amp;
B \arrow[d,"g"]\\
&amp; C
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>commutes when</p>

<p>
<md>g\circ f=h.</md>
</p>

<p>Commutative diagrams allow large collections of equations to be represented
visually.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-functors">
<title>Functors</title>
<p>Categories themselves can be related by structure-preserving maps.</p>

<p>These are called functors.</p>

<definition>
<title>Covariant Functor</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>covariant functor</em></p>

<p>
<md>F:\mathcal C\to\mathcal D</md>
</p>

<p>assigns:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>to every object <m>A</m> of <m>\mathcal C</m>, an object <m>F(A)</m> of
    <m>\mathcal D</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>to every morphism <m>f:A\to B</m>, a morphism</p>

<p>
<md>F(f):F(A)\to F(B),</md>
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>such that identities and composition are preserved.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>F(1_A)=1_{F(A)},</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>F(g\circ f)=F(g)\circ F(f).</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-functor-visual">
<title>Visualizing a Functor</title>
<p>A functor transports an entire diagram from one category to another.</p>

<p>\begin{tikzpicture}[
    box/.style={draw,rounded corners,minimum width=36mm,minimum height=28mm},
    &gt;=stealth
]
\node[box] (C) at (0,0) {<m>\mathcal C:\quad A\xrightarrow{f}B</m>};
\node[box] (D) at (6,0) {<m>\mathcal D:\quad F(A)\xrightarrow{F(f)}F(B)</m>};
\draw[-&gt;,thick] (C)--node[above]{<m>F</m>}(D);
\end{tikzpicture}</p>

<p>The essential point is that the pattern of composition is preserved.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-forgetful-functors">
<title>Forgetful Functors</title>
<p>A common functor discards some structure.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>U:\mathbf{Grp}\to\mathbf{Set}</md>
</p>

<p>sends each group to its underlying set and each group homomorphism to its
underlying function.</p>

<p>The letter <m>U</m> commonly suggests <em>underlying</em> or <em>forgetful</em>.</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>U:\mathbf{Vect}_F\to\mathbf{Set}</md>
</p>

<p>forgets the vector-space operations and remembers only the underlying set.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-free-functors">
<title>Free Constructions as Functors</title>
<p>A set <m>X</m> can be sent to the free group generated by <m>X</m>.</p>

<p>This defines a functor</p>

<p>
<md>F:\mathbf{Set}\to\mathbf{Grp}.</md>
</p>

<p>Likewise one may construct:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>free abelian groups;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>free modules;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>free associative algebras;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>free lattices;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>free universal algebras.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>These constructions are closely related to adjunctions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-identity-functor">
<title>Identity Functor</title>
<p>Every category <m>\mathcal C</m> has an identity functor</p>

<p>
<md>1_{\mathcal C}:\mathcal C\to\mathcal C.</md>
</p>

<p>It sends</p>

<p>
<md>A\mapsto A</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>f\mapsto f.</md>
</p>

<p>Functor composition is associative, and identity functors behave as
identities.</p>

<p>Consequently, categories themselves can be organized into higher-level
categorical structures.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-contravariant-functors">
<title>Contravariant Functors</title>
<p>Some constructions reverse the direction of morphisms.</p>

<definition>
<title>Contravariant Functor</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>contravariant functor</em></p>

<p>
<md>F:\mathcal C\to\mathcal D</md>
</p>

<p>assigns a morphism</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B</md>
</p>

<p>to a morphism</p>

<p>
<md>F(f):F(B)\to F(A),</md>
</p>

<p>reversing the direction of arrows.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Composition is reversed:</p>

<p>
<md>F(g\circ f)=F(f)\circ F(g).</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-opposite-categories">
<title>Opposite Categories</title>
<p>Contravariance can be expressed using an opposite category.</p>

<definition>
<title>Opposite Category</title>
<statement>
<p>The <em>opposite category</em> <m>\mathcal C^{\mathrm{op}}</m> has the same
objects as <m>\mathcal C</m>, but every morphism is reversed.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Thus a morphism</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B</md>
</p>

<p>in <m>\mathcal C</m> becomes a morphism</p>

<p>
<md>f^{\mathrm{op}}:B\to A</md>
</p>

<p>in <m>\mathcal C^{\mathrm{op}}</m>.</p>

<p>The superscript</p>

<p>
<md>\mathrm{op}</md>
</p>

<p>is read <q>opposite.</q></p>

<p>A contravariant functor from <m>\mathcal C</m> to <m>\mathcal D</m> can therefore
be treated as an ordinary covariant functor</p>

<p>
<md>F:\mathcal C^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathcal D.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-dual-space-functor">
<title>Dual Vector Spaces as a Contravariant Example</title>
<p>Let <m>V</m> be a vector space over <m>F</m>.</p>

<p>Its dual space is</p>

<p>
<md>V^*=\operatorname{Hom}_F(V,F).</md>
</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>T:V\to W</md>
</p>

<p>is linear, then the dual map goes in the opposite direction:</p>

<p>
<md>T^*:W^*\to V^*.</md>
</p>

<p>It is defined by</p>

<p>
<md>T^*(\varphi)=\varphi\circ T.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus dualization reverses arrows.</p>

<p>This is a natural example of a contravariant construction.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-natural-transformations">
<title>Natural Transformations</title>
<p>Functors are maps between categories.</p>

<p>Natural transformations are maps between functors.</p>

<definition>
<title>Natural Transformation</title>
<statement>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>F,G:\mathcal C\to\mathcal D</md>
</p>

<p>be functors. A <em>natural transformation</em></p>

<p>
<md>\eta:F\Rightarrow G</md>
</p>

<p>assigns to every object <m>A</m> of <m>\mathcal C</m> a morphism</p>

<p>
<md>\eta_A:F(A)\to G(A)</md>
</p>

<p>such that for every morphism <m>f:A\to B</m>, the naturality square commutes.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\eta</md>
</p>

<p>is the lowercase Greek letter <em>eta</em>, commonly pronounced
<q>ay-tuh</q> or <q>ee-tuh.</q></p>

<p>The double arrow</p>

<p>
<md>\Rightarrow</md>
</p>

<p>indicates a transformation between functors rather than a morphism between
ordinary objects.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-naturality-square">
<title>Naturality Square</title>
<p>The defining condition is represented by</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large,row sep=large]
F(A) \arrow[r,"F(f)"] \arrow[d,"\eta_A"'] &amp;
F(B) \arrow[d,"\eta_B"]\\
G(A) \arrow[r,"G(f)"'] &amp;
G(B)
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>Commutativity means</p>

<p>
<md>\eta_B\circ F(f)=G(f)\circ\eta_A.</md>
</p>

<p>The components <m>\eta_A</m> must therefore interact consistently with every
morphism of <m>\mathcal C</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-natural-isomorphisms">
<title>Natural Isomorphisms</title>
<p>A natural transformation</p>

<p>
<md>\eta:F\Rightarrow G</md>
</p>

<p>is a <em>natural isomorphism</em> if every component</p>

<p>
<md>\eta_A:F(A)\to G(A)</md>
</p>

<p>is an isomorphism.</p>

<p>One then writes</p>

<p>
<md>F\cong G.</md>
</p>

<p>This means that the two functors perform essentially the same construction
in a way compatible with all morphisms.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-natural-isomorphism-example">
<title>A Natural Isomorphism Example</title>
<p>For finite-dimensional vector spaces over <m>F</m>, there is a canonical map</p>

<p>
<md>V\to V^{**}.</md>
</p>

<p>It sends a vector <m>v</m> to the functional</p>

<p>
<md>\widehat v:V^*\to F,\qquad \widehat v(\varphi)=\varphi(v).</md>
</p>

<p>This construction is natural in <m>V</m>.</p>

<p>Thus finite-dimensional vector spaces are naturally isomorphic to their
double duals.</p>

<p>By contrast, an isomorphism</p>

<p>
<md>V\cong V^*</md>
</p>

<p>usually requires choosing a basis or additional structure and is not
canonical in the same way.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-equivalence-categories">
<title>Equivalence of Categories</title>
<p>Two categories may describe essentially the same mathematics without being
literally isomorphic as categories.</p>

<definition>
<title>Equivalence of Categories</title>
<statement>
<p>Categories <m>\mathcal C</m> and <m>\mathcal D</m> are <em>equivalent</em> if there
exist functors</p>

<p>
<md>F:\mathcal C\to\mathcal D,\qquad
G:\mathcal D\to\mathcal C</md>
</p>

<p>together with natural isomorphisms</p>

<p>
<md>G\circ F\cong1_{\mathcal C},\qquad
F\circ G\cong1_{\mathcal D}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>One often writes</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal C\simeq\mathcal D.</md>
</p>

<p>The symbol <m>\simeq</m> is read <q>equivalent to</q> in this context.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-isomorphism-equivalence-categories">
<title>Isomorphism Versus Equivalence of Categories</title>
<p>An isomorphism of categories requires exact inverse functors.</p>

<p>An equivalence allows the composites to be naturally isomorphic to the
identity functors.</p>

<p>Thus equivalence is weaker than literal categorical isomorphism but captures
the notion of having the same categorical structure.</p>

<p>\begin{importantbox}
In category theory, equivalence of categories is often the appropriate
notion of <q>sameness</q> for entire mathematical theories.
\end{importantbox}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-full-faithful">
<title>Full and Faithful Functors</title>
<p>A functor</p>

<p>
<md>F:\mathcal C\to\mathcal D</md>
</p>

<p>induces maps</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C}(A,B)
\to
\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal D}(F(A),F(B)).</md>
</p>

<definition>
<title>Faithful Functor</title>
<statement>
<p>A functor is <em>faithful</em> if each induced map on Hom-sets is injective.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<definition>
<title>Full Functor</title>
<statement>
<p>A functor is <em>full</em> if each induced map on Hom-sets is surjective.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Thus:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>faithful means distinct morphisms remain distinct;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>full means every morphism between images comes from a morphism in
    the original category.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-essentially-surjective">
<title>Essentially Surjective Functors</title>
<p>A functor</p>

<p>
<md>F:\mathcal C\to\mathcal D</md>
</p>

<p>is <em>essentially surjective</em> if every object <m>D</m> of <m>\mathcal D</m>
is isomorphic to some object <m>F(C)</m>.</p>

<p>This is weaker than requiring every object literally to equal an image.</p>

<p>A fundamental criterion states:</p>

<p>\begin{theorem}
A functor is an equivalence of categories if and only if it is full,
faithful, and essentially surjective.
\end{theorem}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-hom-functors">
<title>Hom-Functors</title>
<p>Fix an object <m>A</m> in a category <m>\mathcal C</m>.</p>

<p>The assignment</p>

<p>
<md>X\mapsto\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C}(A,X)</md>
</p>

<p>defines a covariant functor</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C}(A,-):
\mathcal C\to\mathbf{Set}.</md>
</p>

<p>The dash indicates the variable position.</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>X\mapsto\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C}(X,A)</md>
</p>

<p>defines a contravariant functor</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C}(-,A):
\mathcal C^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathbf{Set}.</md>
</p>

<p>These Hom-functors are fundamental to category theory.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-representable-functors">
<title>Representable Functors</title>
<definition>
<title>Representable Functor</title>
<statement>
<p>A functor</p>

<p>
<md>F:\mathcal C\to\mathbf{Set}</md>
</p>

<p>is <em>representable</em> if there exists an object <m>A\in\mathcal C</m> and a
natural isomorphism</p>

<p>
<md>F\cong\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C}(A,-).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The object <m>A</m> is said to <em>represent</em> the functor.</p>

<p>The idea is that information produced by <m>F</m> can be described entirely
through morphisms originating from a single object.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-yoneda-perspective">
<title>The Yoneda Perspective</title>
<p>Category theory suggests that an object can be understood by studying all
morphisms connecting it to other objects.</p>

<p>Instead of asking only</p>

<p>\begin{quote}
What elements does <m>A</m> contain?
\end{quote}</p>

<p>one asks</p>

<p>\begin{quote}
How does <m>A</m> map to and from every other object?
\end{quote}</p>

<p>This principle is formalized by the Yoneda lemma.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-yoneda-lemma">
<title>Yoneda Lemma</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Yoneda Lemma]
Let</p>

<p>
<md>F:\mathcal C\to\mathbf{Set}</md>
</p>

<p>be a functor and let <m>A</m> be an object of <m>\mathcal C</m>. Then there is a
natural bijection</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Nat}
\bigl(\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C}(A,-),F\bigr)
\cong F(A).</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Here</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Nat}(F,G)</md>
</p>

<p>denotes the collection of natural transformations from <m>F</m> to <m>G</m>.</p>

<p>The theorem says that natural transformations from the Hom-functor
represented by <m>A</m> into <m>F</m> correspond exactly to elements of <m>F(A)</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-understanding-yoneda">
<title>Understanding Yoneda</title>
<p>The formula</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Nat}(\operatorname{Hom}(A,-),F)\cong F(A)</md>
</p>

<p>may initially appear abstract.</p>

<p>Its conceptual message is simpler:</p>

<p>\begin{conceptbox}
An object is determined, up to isomorphism, by the pattern of morphisms
connecting it with all other objects.
\end{conceptbox}</p>

<p>This is one of the central philosophical and technical principles of
category theory.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-yoneda-embedding">
<title>Yoneda Embedding</title>
<p>The assignment</p>

<p>
<md>A\mapsto\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C}(-,A)</md>
</p>

<p>defines a functor</p>

<p>
<md>Y:\mathcal C\to
\mathbf{Set}^{\mathcal C^{\mathrm{op}}}.</md>
</p>

<p>This is called the <em>Yoneda embedding</em>.</p>

<p>The category</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{Set}^{\mathcal C^{\mathrm{op}}}</md>
</p>

<p>is a functor category whose objects are functors</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal C^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathbf{Set}.</md>
</p>

<p>The Yoneda embedding is fully faithful.</p>

<p>Thus <m>\mathcal C</m> can be faithfully represented inside a category of
set-valued functors.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-adjunctions">
<title>Adjunctions</title>
<p>Adjunctions describe pairs of functors that are related through a natural
correspondence of morphisms.</p>

<definition>
<title>Adjunction</title>
<statement>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>F:\mathcal C\to\mathcal D,\qquad
G:\mathcal D\to\mathcal C.</md>
</p>

<p>The functor <m>F</m> is <em>left adjoint</em> to <m>G</m>, written</p>

<p>
<md>F\dashv G,</md>
</p>

<p>if there is a natural bijection</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal D}(F(C),D)
\cong
\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C}(C,G(D))</md>
</p>

<p>for all objects <m>C\in\mathcal C</m> and <m>D\in\mathcal D</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\dashv</md>
</p>

<p>is read <q>is left adjoint to.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-adjunction-diagram">
<title>Adjunction Diagram</title>
<p>An adjunction is often pictured as</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=huge]
\mathcal C
\arrow[r,bend left=20,"F"]
&amp;
\mathcal D
\arrow[l,bend left=20,"G"]
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>with</p>

<p>
<md>F\dashv G.</md>
</p>

<p>The functors generally are not inverses.</p>

<p>Instead, they are linked through the natural Hom-set correspondence.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-free-forgetful-adjunction">
<title>Free and Forgetful Adjunction</title>
<p>One of the most important examples is the free-group functor</p>

<p>
<md>F:\mathbf{Set}\to\mathbf{Grp}</md>
</p>

<p>and the forgetful functor</p>

<p>
<md>U:\mathbf{Grp}\to\mathbf{Set}.</md>
</p>

<p>They satisfy</p>

<p>
<md>F\dashv U.</md>
</p>

<p>The adjunction gives a natural bijection</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbf{Grp}}(F(X),G)
\cong
\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbf{Set}}(X,U(G)).</md>
</p>

<p>This says that a group homomorphism from the free group on <m>X</m> is uniquely
determined by an arbitrary function from the generating set <m>X</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-adjunctions-galois">
<title>Adjunctions and Galois Connections</title>
<p>Recall from lattice theory that a Galois connection between posets satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>f(p)\leq q\iff p\leq g(q).</md>
</p>

<p>When posets are viewed as categories, this becomes precisely the
order-theoretic version of an adjunction.</p>

<p>Thus the categorical relation</p>

<p>
<md>F\dashv G</md>
</p>

<p>generalizes the Galois connections of order theory.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-units-counits">
<title>Units and Counits</title>
<p>Every adjunction</p>

<p>
<md>F\dashv G</md>
</p>

<p>determines natural transformations called the <em>unit</em> and
<em>counit</em>.</p>

<p>The unit is</p>

<p>
<md>\eta:1_{\mathcal C}\Rightarrow G\circ F.</md>
</p>

<p>The counit is</p>

<p>
<md>\varepsilon:F\circ G\Rightarrow1_{\mathcal D}.</md>
</p>

<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\varepsilon</md>
</p>

<p>is the lowercase Greek letter <em>epsilon</em>, pronounced <q>ep-si-lon.</q></p>

<p>These transformations satisfy compatibility conditions called the
<em>triangle identities</em>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-triangle-identities">
<title>Triangle Identities</title>
<p>For an adjunction <m>F\dashv G</m>, the unit and counit satisfy</p>

<p>
<md>\varepsilon_{F(C)}\circ F(\eta_C)=1_{F(C)},</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>G(\varepsilon_D)\circ\eta_{G(D)}=1_{G(D)}.</md>
</p>

<p>These identities ensure that the unit and counit encode the same adjunction
as the Hom-set bijection.</p>

<p>Detailed manipulation of adjunctions belongs to more advanced categorical
study, but recognizing the structure is essential.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-diagrams-shapes">
<title>Diagrams and Shapes</title>
<p>A categorical diagram is formally a functor</p>

<p>
<md>D:\mathcal J\to\mathcal C,</md>
</p>

<p>where <m>\mathcal J</m> is an indexing category describing the shape of the
diagram.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}
A \arrow[r] &amp; B \arrow[r] &amp; C
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>has the shape of three objects connected sequentially.</p>

<p>A diagram</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}
&amp; B\\
A \arrow[ur] \arrow[dr] &amp;\\
&amp; C
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>has a different shape.</p>

<p>Limits and colimits depend on these diagram shapes.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-cones">
<title>Cones</title>
<p>Given a diagram <m>D:\mathcal J\to\mathcal C</m>, a <em>cone</em> to <m>D</m>
consists of an object <m>N</m> together with compatible morphisms from <m>N</m>
to the objects of the diagram.</p>

<p>One may picture <m>N</m> as sitting above the diagram:</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=large,column sep=large]
&amp; N \arrow[dl] \arrow[d] \arrow[dr] &amp;\\
A \arrow[r] &amp; B \arrow[r] &amp; C
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>The compatibility conditions require the triangles determined by the
diagram to commute.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-limits">
<title>Limits</title>
<definition>
<title>Limit</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>limit</em> of a diagram is a universal cone to that diagram: every other
cone factors uniquely through it.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The limit of a diagram <m>D</m> is commonly denoted</p>

<p>
<md>\varprojlim D</md>
</p>

<p>or</p>

<p>
<md>\lim D.</md>
</p>

<p>Here <m>\varprojlim</m> is read <q>inverse limit</q> or simply <q>limit,</q> depending
on context.</p>

<p>Limits unify many constructions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-examples-limits">
<title>Examples of Limits</title>
<p>Several familiar constructions are categorical limits.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>

<p>Thus products are not isolated constructions; they are special cases of a
single general concept.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-equalizers">
<title>Equalizers</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>f,g:A\to B.</md>
</p>

<p>An <em>equalizer</em> is a morphism</p>

<p>
<md>e:E\to A</md>
</p>

<p>such that</p>

<p>
<md>f\circ e=g\circ e,</md>
</p>

<p>and it is universal among morphisms having this property.</p>

<p>Diagrammatically,</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}
E \arrow[r,"e"] &amp;
A \arrow[r,shift left=3pt,"f"]
\arrow[r,shift right=3pt,"g"'] &amp;
B
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>In <m>\mathbf{Set}</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>E=\{a\in A:f(a)=g(a)\}.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-pullbacks">
<title>Pullbacks</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to C,\qquad g:B\to C.</md>
</p>

<p>Their pullback is an object often written</p>

<p>
<md>A\times_C B.</md>
</p>

<p>It fits into a commutative square</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=large,column sep=large]
A\times_C B \arrow[r] \arrow[d] &amp;
B \arrow[d,"g"]\\
A \arrow[r,"f"'] &amp;
C
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>and satisfies a universal property.</p>

<p>In <m>\mathbf{Set}</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>A\times_C B=\{(a,b)\in A\times B:f(a)=g(b)\}.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-cocones-colimits">
<title>Cocones and Colimits</title>
<p>The dual of a cone is a <em>cocone</em>.</p>

<p>Instead of morphisms pointing from a common object into a diagram, the
diagram maps into a common object.</p>

<definition>
<title>Colimit</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>colimit</em> is a universal cocone from a diagram.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The colimit of <m>D</m> is commonly written</p>

<p>
<md>\varinjlim D</md>
</p>

<p>or</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{colim}D.</md>
</p>

<p>The symbol <m>\varinjlim</m> is read <q>direct limit</q> or <q>colimit,</q> depending
on context.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-examples-colimits">
<title>Examples of Colimits</title>
<p>Colimits include:</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>

<p>The prefix <q>co-</q> usually indicates a construction obtained by reversing
arrows.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-coequalizers">
<title>Coequalizers</title>
<p>Given</p>

<p>
<md>f,g:A\to B,</md>
</p>

<p>a coequalizer is a morphism</p>

<p>
<md>q:B\to Q</md>
</p>

<p>such that</p>

<p>
<md>q\circ f=q\circ g,</md>
</p>

<p>and it is universal among morphisms satisfying this equality.</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}
A \arrow[r,shift left=3pt,"f"]
\arrow[r,shift right=3pt,"g"'] &amp;
B \arrow[r,"q"] &amp;
Q
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>In algebraic categories, coequalizers often resemble quotient constructions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-pushouts">
<title>Pushouts</title>
<p>Given</p>

<p>
<md>f:C\to A,\qquad g:C\to B,</md>
</p>

<p>their pushout fits into a square</p>

<p>\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=large,column sep=large]
C \arrow[r,"g"] \arrow[d,"f"'] &amp;
B \arrow[d]\\
A \arrow[r] &amp;
A\amalg_C B
\end{tikzcd}</p>

<p>satisfying a universal property.</p>

<p>Pushouts can be interpreted as ways of gluing objects together along a
shared piece.</p>

<p>This becomes especially important in topology and algebraic geometry.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-limits-colimits-unified">
<title>Limits and Colimits as a Unified Language</title>
<p>The categorical viewpoint organizes many apparently unrelated constructions.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>

<p>Each item in the right column is categorically dual to the corresponding
item in the left column.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-categorical-kernels">
<title>Kernels as Categorical Constructions</title>
<p>In categories with suitable zero morphisms, a kernel can be characterized
through a universal property.</p>

<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B,</md>
</p>

<p>the kernel is a morphism</p>

<p>
<md>k:K\to A</md>
</p>

<p>satisfying</p>

<p>
<md>f\circ k=0,</md>
</p>

<p>through which every morphism annihilated by <m>f</m> factors uniquely.</p>

<p>This recovers familiar kernels from linear algebra, group theory, and module
theory within a common categorical framework.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-categorical-cokernels">
<title>Cokernels</title>
<p>The dual construction is the cokernel.</p>

<p>A cokernel of</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B</md>
</p>

<p>is a morphism</p>

<p>
<md>q:B\to Q</md>
</p>

<p>satisfying</p>

<p>
<md>q\circ f=0</md>
</p>

<p>and a corresponding universal property.</p>

<p>Cokernels naturally produce quotient-like objects.</p>

<p>They become especially important in homological algebra.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-additive-categories">
<title>Additive Categories</title>
<p>Some categories carry additional algebraic structure on their Hom-sets.</p>

<p>An <em>additive category</em> is, roughly, a category in which:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>each Hom-set is an abelian group;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>composition is bilinear;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>finite products and coproducts agree as biproducts.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>The category</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{Ab}</md>
</p>

<p>is additive.</p>

<p>So is</p>

<p>
<md>R\text{-}\mathbf{Mod}.</md>
</p>

<p>Additive categories provide the natural environment for much of homological
algebra.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-biproducts">
<title>Biproducts</title>
<p>In an additive category, a finite product and coproduct may coincide.</p>

<p>The resulting object is called a <em>biproduct</em>.</p>

<p>For modules,</p>

<p>
<md>M\oplus N</md>
</p>

<p>is simultaneously a categorical product and coproduct.</p>

<p>This explains categorically why direct sums possess both projection maps</p>

<p>
<md>M\oplus N\to M,\qquad M\oplus N\to N,</md>
</p>

<p>and inclusion maps</p>

<p>
<md>M\to M\oplus N,\qquad N\to M\oplus N.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-abelian-categories">
<title>Abelian Categories</title>
<p>An <em>abelian category</em> is an additive category with sufficiently
well-behaved kernels, cokernels, monomorphisms, and epimorphisms.</p>

<p>The full axiomatic definition is deferred to homological algebra.</p>

<p>The canonical examples are</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{Ab}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>R\text{-}\mathbf{Mod}.</md>
</p>

<p>Abelian categories provide the abstract setting in which exact sequences
and homological constructions behave similarly to module theory.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-homological-algebra">
<title>Category Theory and Homological Algebra</title>
<p>Homological algebra uses category theory extensively.</p>

<p>Concepts such as</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>chain complexes;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>exact sequences;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>projective and injective objects;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>derived functors;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\operatorname{Ext}</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\operatorname{Tor}</m>;</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>are most naturally expressed categorically.</p>

<p>The detailed theory remains in <xref ref="sec-homological-algebra" />; the purpose
here is to establish the categorical language needed to interpret it.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-representation-theory">
<title>Category Theory and Representation Theory</title>
<p>Representation theory can also be formulated categorically.</p>

<p>A representation of a group <m>G</m> on vector spaces can be viewed as a
functor from the one-object category associated with <m>G</m> into</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{Vect}_F.</md>
</p>

<p>The group elements become invertible linear transformations.</p>

<p>Natural transformations between such functors correspond to intertwining
maps between representations.</p>

<p>Thus functorial language provides a unified description of representations.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-topology-connection">
<title>Category Theory and Topology</title>
<p>Topology provides many important functors.</p>

<p>For example, the fundamental group construction associates a group to a
pointed topological space:</p>

<p>
<md>(X,x_0)\mapsto\pi_1(X,x_0).</md>
</p>

<p>Continuous maps induce group homomorphisms.</p>

<p>Similarly, homology associates algebraic objects to topological spaces.</p>

<p>Category theory explains why such constructions preserve structural
relationships and why topological information can be transported into
algebra.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-geometry">
<title>Category Theory and Geometry</title>
<p>Modern geometry relies heavily on categorical language.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>categories of manifolds;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>categories of schemes;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>sheaves;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>fiber products;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>functors of points;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>derived categories;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>stacks.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>These topics belong to later geometry and topology chapters, but their
language is built from the categorical foundations developed here.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-logic">
<title>Category Theory and Logic</title>
<p>Category theory also interacts deeply with logic.</p>

<p>Categorical structures can model:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>logical propositions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>proofs;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>type systems;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>constructive logic;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>computation.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>These connections become important in mathematical logic, type theory, and
the foundations of mathematics.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-computer-science">
<title>Category Theory and Computer Science</title>
<p>Categorical ideas have applications in theoretical computer science,
including:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>programming-language semantics;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>functional programming;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>type theory;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compositional systems;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>databases;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>formal verification;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>concurrency.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>The emphasis on composition makes category theory especially useful for
describing systems built from interacting components.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-universal-construction-pattern">
<title>Universal Constructions as a Pattern</title>
<p>Many categorical definitions share the same structure.</p>

<p>\begin{tikzpicture}[
    node distance=9mm,
    box/.style={draw,rounded corners,align=center,inner sep=5pt},
    &gt;=stealth
]
\node[box] (problem) {A mapping problem};
\node[box,below=of problem] (object) {A distinguished object};
\node[box,below=of object] (factor) {Every solution factors through it};
\node[box,below=of factor] (unique) {The factorization is unique};</p>

<p>\draw[-&gt;] (problem)--(object);
\draw[-&gt;] (object)--(factor);
\draw[-&gt;] (factor)--(unique);
\end{tikzpicture}</p>

<p>This pattern appears in:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>products;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>coproducts;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>free objects;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>quotient constructions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>tensor products;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>kernels;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>cokernels;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>limits;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>colimits;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>adjunctions.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Learning to recognize universal properties is one of the central skills of
category theory.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-worked-initial-group">
<title>Worked Example: Initial Object in Groups</title>
<example>
<title>The Trivial Group as an Initial Object</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>E=\{e\}</md>
</p>

<p>be the trivial group. Show that <m>E</m> is initial in <m>\mathbf{Grp}</m>.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{\{e\}\text{ is an initial object of }\mathbf{Grp}.}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-worked-zero-vector-space">
<title>Worked Example: Zero Object in Vector Spaces</title>
<example>
<title>The Zero Vector Space</title>
<p>Show that the zero vector space <m>\{0\}</m> is a zero object in
<m>\mathbf{Vect}_F</m>.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{\{0\}\text{ is a zero object in }\mathbf{Vect}_F.}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-worked-functoriality">
<title>Worked Example: Functoriality</title>
<example>
<title>Checking a Forgetful Functor</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>U:\mathbf{Grp}\to\mathbf{Set}.</md>
</p>

<p>Verify that <m>U</m> preserves composition.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{U(g\circ f)=U(g)\circ U(f).}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-common-errors">
<title>Common Errors</title>
<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-morphisms-must-be-functions">
<title>Assuming Morphisms Must Be Functions</title>
<p>Many concrete categories use functions as morphisms, but the abstract
definition of a category does not require morphisms to be set-theoretic
functions.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-reversing-composition-order">
<title>Reversing Composition Order</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>A\xrightarrow{f}B\xrightarrow{g}C,</md>
</p>

<p>the composite is</p>

<p>
<md>g\circ f,</md>
</p>

<p>not <m>f\circ g</m>.</p>

<p>The morphism <m>f</m> acts first.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-forgetting-identity-morphisms">
<title>Forgetting Identity Morphisms</title>
<p>Every object must possess an identity morphism.</p>

<p>A directed graph with associative-looking paths is not automatically a
category unless identities and composition are properly defined.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-isomorphic-with-equal">
<title>Confusing Isomorphic with Equal</title>
<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>A\cong B</md>
</p>

<p>does not generally mean</p>

<p>
<md>A=B.</md>
</p>

<p>It means the objects are structurally equivalent within the category.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-monomorphism-means-injective">
<title>Assuming Monomorphism Means Injective</title>
<p>This is true in <m>\mathbf{Set}</m> and many familiar algebraic categories, but
the categorical definition is based on cancellation.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-epimorphism-means-surjective">
<title>Assuming Epimorphism Means Surjective</title>
<p>Epimorphisms are categorically right-cancellable morphisms.</p>

<p>They need not be surjective in every category.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-treating-universal-objects-as-literally-unique">
<title>Treating Universal Objects as Literally Unique</title>
<p>Products, coproducts, initial objects, terminal objects, and other universal
constructions are generally unique only up to unique isomorphism.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-products-with-coproducts">
<title>Confusing Products with Coproducts</title>
<p>Products receive maps from a common source.</p>

<p>Coproducts send maps into a common target.</p>

<p>Their universal arrows point in opposite directions.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-functors-with-natural-transformations">
<title>Confusing Functors with Natural Transformations</title>
<p>A functor maps between categories.</p>

<p>A natural transformation maps between functors.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-ignoring-naturality">
<title>Ignoring Naturality</title>
<p>A family of morphisms</p>

<p>
<md>\eta_A:F(A)\to G(A)</md>
</p>

<p>does not automatically form a natural transformation.</p>

<p>The naturality square must commute for every morphism.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-contravariance-with-inversion">
<title>Confusing Contravariance with Inversion</title>
<p>A contravariant functor reverses morphism directions.</p>

<p>It does not necessarily send morphisms to inverses.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-equivalent-categories-are-isomorphic">
<title>Assuming Equivalent Categories Are Isomorphic</title>
<p>Equivalence is weaker than literal categorical isomorphism.</p>

<p>It identifies categories that encode essentially the same structural
information.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-treating-an-adjunction-as-an-equivalence">
<title>Treating an Adjunction as an Equivalence</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>F\dashv G,</md>
</p>

<p>the functors need not be inverses and the categories need not be equivalent.</p>

<p>Adjunction expresses a natural correspondence of Hom-sets.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-memorizing-limits-without-universal-properties">
<title>Memorizing Limits Without Universal Properties</title>
<p>A product, pullback, equalizer, or other limit is not defined merely by its
concrete formula in one category.</p>

<p>Its categorical meaning comes from its universal property.</p>
</subsubsection>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-applications">
<title>Applications and Connections</title>
<p>\begin{applicationbox}
<em>Algebra.</em>
Groups, rings, modules, fields, lattices, and other algebraic structures
form categories whose morphisms preserve the relevant operations.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Universal Algebra.</em>
Free algebras, homomorphisms, congruences, and varieties have natural
categorical formulations.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Representation Theory.</em>
Representations can be viewed as functors into categories of vector spaces
or modules.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Homological Algebra.</em>
Exact sequences, chain complexes, derived functors, projective objects,
injective objects, <m>\operatorname{Ext}</m>, and <m>\operatorname{Tor}</m> rely
heavily on categorical language.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Topology.</em>
Fundamental groups and homology are functorial constructions that translate
topological spaces and continuous maps into algebraic objects and
homomorphisms.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Geometry.</em>
Products, fiber products, sheaves, schemes, and many modern geometric
constructions are most naturally formulated categorically.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Logic and Foundations.</em>
Categories provide models of logical systems, type theories, and
constructive mathematics.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Computer Science.</em>
Functors, natural transformations, monads, adjunctions, and fixed-point
constructions appear in programming-language semantics and functional
programming.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Mathematical Organization.</em>
Category theory provides a common language for recognizing when different
branches of mathematics are using the same structural construction under
different names.
\end{applicationbox}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-16-category-theory-subsec-category-exercises">
